{
  "source": "../../scripts/color-taylor-script-cut-03.md",
  "wpm": 150,
  "gaps": {
    "line": 0.5,
    "paragraph": 1.2,
    "beat": 2.5
  },
  "lead": 3,
  "tail": 3,
  "lines": [
    {
      "id": "1.1",
      "beat": 1,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "Hey, I'm Taylor.",
      "start": 3,
      "end": 4.2,
      "gapBefore": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "1.1",
      "beat": 1,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "I'm a real human.",
      "start": 4.7,
      "end": 6.3,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "1.2",
      "beat": 1,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "I'm also a user experience and product designer, and I'd like to show you something I've been working on.",
      "start": 7.5,
      "end": 15.1,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "1.3",
      "beat": 1,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "It's called Color Taylor.",
      "start": 15.6,
      "end": 17.2,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "2.1",
      "beat": 2,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "I've always been interested in, but struggled with, how red, green, and blue are used to make the colors on a display.",
      "start": 19.7,
      "end": 28.5,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "2.2",
      "beat": 2,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "Hue, saturation, and brightness, and hue, saturation, and lightness are two approaches that make more sense to me, from working with color wheels for traditional art and design.",
      "start": 29.7,
      "end": 40.9,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "2.3",
      "beat": 2,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "Our focus for this presentation will be HSB.",
      "start": 41.4,
      "end": 44.6,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "2.4",
      "beat": 2,
      "line": 4,
      "text": "Color Taylor is meant to be a playground and educational tool for how RGB and HSB work together.",
      "start": 45.8,
      "end": 53,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "2.5",
      "beat": 2,
      "line": 5,
      "text": "You see the relationships in real time, to help better understand how colors work on your display.",
      "start": 53.5,
      "end": 60.3,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "2.6",
      "beat": 2,
      "line": 6,
      "text": "I'll just move myself out of the way.",
      "start": 61.5,
      "end": 64.7,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "3.1",
      "beat": 3,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "The color editor on the right should be familiar to most people who have worked with creative tools.",
      "start": 67.2,
      "end": 74.4,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "3.2",
      "beat": 3,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "The hexagon on the left is a different way of displaying the same information.",
      "start": 75.6,
      "end": 81.2,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "3.3",
      "beat": 3,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "Hue is an angle that ranges from 0 to 360 degrees.",
      "start": 82.4,
      "end": 86.8,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "3.4",
      "beat": 3,
      "line": 4,
      "text": "Red is 0...",
      "start": 88,
      "end": 89.2,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "3.4",
      "beat": 3,
      "line": 4,
      "text": "green is 120...",
      "start": 89.7,
      "end": 90.9,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "3.4",
      "beat": 3,
      "line": 4,
      "text": "and blue is 240.",
      "start": 91.4,
      "end": 93,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "3.5",
      "beat": 3,
      "line": 5,
      "text": "The difference is that red, green, and blue each get their own direction on the hex, matching where they sit on the hue wheel.",
      "start": 94.2,
      "end": 103.8,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "3.6",
      "beat": 3,
      "line": 6,
      "text": "And in between each of these we see combinations: yellow, cyan, and magenta.",
      "start": 105,
      "end": 110.2,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "3.7",
      "beat": 3,
      "line": 7,
      "text": "Not to be confused with CMYK, which works differently and is for another time.",
      "start": 111.4,
      "end": 117,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "4.1",
      "beat": 4,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "Let's take an orange as a reference point, 30 degrees.",
      "start": 119.5,
      "end": 123.5,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "4.2",
      "beat": 4,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "When the hue is set to 30 degrees, I know that's going to be orange, because it's between red and yellow, and yellow is between red and green.",
      "start": 124.7,
      "end": 135.9,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "4.3",
      "beat": 4,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "So it's a combination of 2 parts red, 1 part green, 0 parts blue.",
      "start": 136.4,
      "end": 142,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "5.1",
      "beat": 5,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "Adding saturation increases the distance between red, green, and blue.",
      "start": 144.5,
      "end": 148.5,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "5.2",
      "beat": 5,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "Desaturating reduces the distance proportionally between these.",
      "start": 149.7,
      "end": 152.5,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "5.3",
      "beat": 5,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "Saturation works off the highest value.",
      "start": 153.7,
      "end": 156.1,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "5.3",
      "beat": 5,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "Red holds still, and green and blue move toward it or away from it.",
      "start": 156.6,
      "end": 162.2,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "5.4",
      "beat": 5,
      "line": 4,
      "text": "When all three are equal, there's no saturation, and you're left with gray.",
      "start": 163.4,
      "end": 168.6,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "5.5",
      "beat": 5,
      "line": 5,
      "text": "Notice the hue didn't change, we're still at 30, which is orange.",
      "start": 169.8,
      "end": 174.6,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "5.5",
      "beat": 5,
      "line": 5,
      "text": "Same hue, only the distance between RGB values changed.",
      "start": 175.1,
      "end": 178.7,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "6.1",
      "beat": 6,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "Brightness is determined by the highest color value.",
      "start": 181.2,
      "end": 184.4,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "6.2",
      "beat": 6,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "As we scale the brightness, the hue and saturation are maintained by scaling the RGB values proportionately to each other.",
      "start": 185.6,
      "end": 193.6,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "6.3",
      "beat": 6,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "Brightness works off zero.",
      "start": 194.8,
      "end": 196.4,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "6.3",
      "beat": 6,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "All three channels scale together, so the ratios between them stay the same.",
      "start": 196.9,
      "end": 202.1,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "6.4",
      "beat": 6,
      "line": 4,
      "text": "At a hundred percent brightness the hex is at its largest,",
      "start": 203.3,
      "end": 207.7,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "6.5",
      "beat": 6,
      "line": 5,
      "text": "and lowering it shrinks the size of the hex.",
      "start": 208.2,
      "end": 211.8,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "6.6",
      "beat": 6,
      "line": 6,
      "text": "Notice we stay at the hue 30 orange while moving brightness.",
      "start": 212.3,
      "end": 216.7,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "7.1",
      "beat": 7,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "We'll use the swatches section to show how we can use HSB to set six different tints of the same hue.",
      "start": 219.2,
      "end": 227.6,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "7.2",
      "beat": 7,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "We can use HSB to come up with a bright desaturated orange, then go down the scale until we have a darker one.",
      "start": 228.8,
      "end": 238,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "7.3",
      "beat": 7,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "This is really hinting at what a design system does.",
      "start": 239.2,
      "end": 243.2,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "8.1",
      "beat": 8,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "We've been exploring the playground, but if you want the classroom perspective, check out the equations section.",
      "start": 245.7,
      "end": 252.5,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "9.1",
      "beat": 9,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "Now we can move on to the demo, which gives you an outline of some of the things to look for in the Color Taylor app.",
      "start": 255,
      "end": 265.4,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "9.2",
      "beat": 9,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "You can find this demo the first time loading the app,",
      "start": 266.6,
      "end": 271,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "9.3",
      "beat": 9,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "or from the question mark button at the top of the app.",
      "start": 272.2,
      "end": 277,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "10.1",
      "beat": 10,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "Start with the sliders and controls you already know.",
      "start": 279.5,
      "end": 283.1,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "10.2",
      "beat": 10,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "Moving one slider will highlight every other slider it affects.",
      "start": 284.3,
      "end": 288.3,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "10.3",
      "beat": 10,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "Click the droplet button to switch between Source or Mixed colored sliders.",
      "start": 289.5,
      "end": 294.3,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "10.4",
      "beat": 10,
      "line": 4,
      "text": "Click the tags button to show HTML colors on the hex.",
      "start": 295.5,
      "end": 299.9,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "10.5",
      "beat": 10,
      "line": 5,
      "text": "Drag a handle on the hex and watch the RGB and HSB sliders follow.",
      "start": 301.1,
      "end": 306.7,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "10.6",
      "beat": 10,
      "line": 6,
      "text": "Having fun is the goal of the tool.",
      "start": 307.9,
      "end": 311.1,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "11.1",
      "beat": 11,
      "line": 1,
      "text": "If you use Figma, I've also made a Color Taylor plugin that you might like to check out.",
      "start": 313.6,
      "end": 320.8,
      "gapBefore": 2.5
    },
    {
      "id": "11.2",
      "beat": 11,
      "line": 2,
      "text": "That's Color Taylor!",
      "start": 322,
      "end": 323.2,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "11.3",
      "beat": 11,
      "line": 3,
      "text": "Go play with it, and I hope it helps you see the relationship between RGB and HSB in a different light.",
      "start": 324.4,
      "end": 332.8,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "11.4",
      "beat": 11,
      "line": 4,
      "text": "If it's useful, I'd like to hear from you.",
      "start": 334,
      "end": 337.6,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    },
    {
      "id": "11.4",
      "beat": 11,
      "line": 4,
      "text": "Questions and feedback are welcome.",
      "start": 338.1,
      "end": 340.1,
      "gapBefore": 0.5
    },
    {
      "id": "11.5",
      "beat": 11,
      "line": 5,
      "text": "Thanks, see ya.",
      "start": 341.3,
      "end": 342.5,
      "gapBefore": 1.2
    }
  ],
